timedatectl

Find Your Desired Timezone

timedatectl list-timezones
timedatectl list-timezones | grep Asia/Kolkata
sudo timedatectl set-timezone Asia/Kolkata

verify change :
timedatectl

----------------------------

at jobs run as detached background processes, their terminal output is handled differently:

The at command in Linux is a powerful utility used to schedule command or script executions exactly once at a specified future time. Unlike the more common cron utility—which is designed for recurring, periodic tasks—at is the go-to tool for delayed, one-off system maintenance jobs, temporary automation, or single-use workflows.

1. Underlying Architecture & The atd Daemon

For the at command to work, a background system service called the atd daemon must be actively running.
systemctl enable atd  ;this will start atd at boot time
systemctl start atd  ;this will manually start atd

• How it works: When you schedule a job using at, the command captures your environment variables, current working directory, and the target execution instructions. It saves this payload as a shell script in the spool directory (typically /var/spool/cron/atjobs/ or /var/spool/at/).

• Execution: The atd daemon wakes up periodically (usually every 60 seconds) to check this folder. If the system clock matches or exceeds the execution timestamp of a queued job, atd runs the job in the background as the user who created it, utilizing the exact environment captured during creation.


2. Basic Syntax and Time Specifications

The fundamental syntax for scheduling a task is:

at [options] time_specification


Absolute Time	at 15:30 
                at 04:00 PM	       Runs at the next occurrence of that exact time (today or tomorrow).


Relative Time	at now + 45 minutes 
                at now + 3 days	            Runs relative to the exact moment you execute the command.



Specific Dates	at 8:00 AM 12/25/2026 
                at 10:00 tomorrow	        ombines exact hours with specific calendar dates or timeline terms.

                at now + 15 days
                at now + 1 week
                at now + 2 years


3. How to Create and Schedule Jobs


Method A: Interactive Mode (Standard Input)

Typing at followed by a time forces the shell into an interactive sub-prompt (at>). You type your sequence of commands, pressing Enter after each one. Once finished, press Ctrl + D to save and exit.

$ at 23:00
at> tar -czf /backups/site.tar.gz /var/www/html
at> systemctl restart nginx
at> <EOT>  # Triggers when you press Ctrl+D
job 14 at Fri Nov  1 23:00:00 2026


Method B: Piping

For quick automation, you can pipeline an string directly into the utility via standard input:

echo "bash /opt/scripts/cleanup.sh" | at now + 2 hours


Method C: Executing Pre-written Scripts

If you want to queue an existing file script directly, use the -f flag:

at 3:00 AM tomorrow -f /home/admin/database_purge.sh


---------------

When you schedule a task with at, it doesn't just save your command in a text file; it creates a complete, self-contained execution package. This design solves a classic problem in automation: "It worked when I typed it, but it failed when the system ran it automatically."

When you are logged into a Linux terminal, your shell holds dozens of invisible configuration details called environment variables. These include things like:
• PATH: The list of folders the system searches through to find commands.
• USER and HOME: Who you are and where your personal files live.
• Custom variables you or your apps have loaded (like JAVA_HOME or database tokens).

If a scheduled script runs in the background without these variables, it will often crash because it cannot find the commands or configurations it needs. To prevent this, at takes a complete snapshot of every single environment variable active in your terminal at that exact second.

at takes your environment, your directory location, and your target commands, and bundles them into a single shell script.
If you navigate into the spool directory (like /var/spool/at/) as the root user and open one of these generated job files, you will see a file structured like this:

#!/bin/sh
# This file was generated automatically by at

# 1. The Environment Snapshot (Hundreds of lines looking like this):
export PATH="/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin"
export USER="john"
export HOME="/home/john"
export LANG="en_US.UTF-8"


to remove a scheduled job use atrm

use job ID along with remove command

atrm 5